---
import {METADATA, LINKS, CAREER} from "../lib/constants";

interface Props {
    title: string;
    description: string;
}

const {title, description} = Astro.props;

/*
 * The body's height floor below is a MINIMUM, deliberately, and the distinction
 * is load-bearing. It used to be an exact viewport height from the medium
 * breakpoint up, which locked the page to 100vh a full breakpoint below where
 * the single-screen bento layout is actually defined — that contract lives on
 * <main> in index.astro and is written against the large breakpoint only.
 *
 * Between 768px and 1023px the grid is just two columns, so the same content is
 * roughly 1160px tall. Against an exact 100vh body the implicit `auto` rows were
 * compressed to fit the viewport and every card's own clipping ate the
 * remainder: at 768x900 four of the eight cards lost content — the intro card
 * 98.45px, its entire row of controls; the Now card 9.23px, the final line of its
 * copy; and each goal card 5.86px, the "last year's" line. Two cards from 800 to
 * 1023px, worst 76.13px down to 62.58px, and one at 768x1024 losing 36.61px.
 * Measured against each card's padding box, which is what it clips to.
 *
 * Nothing scrolled, because the body was exactly as tall as the viewport, so the
 * loss was silent and unrecoverable rather than merely below the fold.
 *
 * A floor fixes that: the body still stretches to the full viewport, so the
 * centring below still centres, and <main> is still capped at the large
 * breakpoint. Where content genuinely needs more room the page now grows and
 * scrolls instead of clipping. Keep this a floor — restoring an exact height
 * reintroduces the defect at every viewport shorter than the content.
 *
 * "Grows instead of clipping" is about the PAGE, and it used to come with a caveat:
 * a fixed max-height on a box INSIDE a card still turns growth into clipping
 * however tall the page is allowed to become. The intro card's copy column carried
 * such a cap, and under text-only zoom at the narrowest medium width it still bit —
 * at 768x900 the cards clipping went 4 -> 0 at a 16px root and 6 -> 0 at 20px, but
 * at a 24px root one card still lost 48px of its control row, against seven cards
 * and 241.5px before. Better by an order of magnitude, not zero.
 *
 * That residue is closed now, and closed at its source rather than here. `main`'s
 * height budget and every breakpoint are font-relative (index.astro and
 * uno.config.ts), so the grid grows with the reader's text and this floor lets the
 * result scroll; the copy column's cap went font-relative in the same pass, and
 * `tests/card-fill.test.ts` now forbids an absolute one anywhere inside a card.
 * Swept as ink past each card's BOTTOM clip edge: 0 at every viewport from 320 to 2560
 * wide, at every root size from 16px to 40px. Name the edge — the right edge was NOT zero
 * when that sweep was published, and it took its own change to close (the control-row
 * note further down this file, and index.astro). A card grows vertically with its content
 * and never horizontally, which is the whole reason the two edges needed separate work.
 *
 * It also fixes the large breakpoint, which the exact height broke as well, and
 * an earlier version of this comment wrongly claimed it left untouched. <main>
 * carries a minimum height of its own at that breakpoint (index.astro owns the
 * number — do not copy it here, it moves), so on a viewport shorter than that
 * minimum the exact-height body could not contain it; because the body centres
 * its children, the overflow was split top and bottom and the top half sat above
 * the scroll origin, where no scrolling reaches. Measured on production against
 * the value in force then: 94px of the first card unreachable at 1024x500, 69 at
 * 550, 44 at 600, 19 at 650, none from 700 up, and the same at every width from
 * 1024 to 1920 — a function of height alone. A laptop-sized bug, not an edge
 * case. Zero unreachable at all of those now.
 * At or above that minimum the large-breakpoint geometry is unchanged, rect for
 * rect.
 *
 * tests/page-fit.test.ts pins it. Utility names are described rather than
 * spelled here on purpose: UnoCSS scans this file, so writing one would emit a
 * real CSS rule that no element wears.
 */
const image = `${Astro.url.origin}/preview.jpg`;

const schema = {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Person",
    name: METADATA.name,
    url: METADATA.site_url,
    sameAs: LINKS.filter(item => /^https?:\/\//.test(item.link)).map(item => item.link),
    image: METADATA.image_url,
    jobTitle: CAREER[0].job_name,
    worksFor: {
        "@type": "Organization", name: CAREER[0].company, address: {
            "@type": "PostalAddress",
            addressLocality: METADATA.address_locality,
            addressCountry: METADATA.address_country,
        },
    },
    nationality: {
        "@type": "Country", name: METADATA.address_locality,
    },
};
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/>
    <link rel="icon" type="image/x-icon" href="/favicon.ico"/>
    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator}/>
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description}/>
    <meta name="robots" content="index, follow"/>
    {/* Basic OG tags for sharing your website's content on platforms like Facebook and LinkedIn */}
    <meta property="og:title" content={title}/>
    <meta property="og:description" content={description}/>
    <meta property="og:type" content="website"/>
    {/* THE PAGE'S OWN URL, not the origin. It was the origin from plan 002, when there
         was one page and the two were the same thing modulo a trailing slash. With four
         routes that decision inverted into a defect: every /patches page told a social
         card it WAS the home page, contradicting the canonical link three lines up, so a
         shared link to /patches/cycling unfurled as calvin.sg. Found by a review panel;
         `Astro.url.href` is what the canonical already uses, so the two now agree by
         construction rather than by coincidence. */}
    <meta property="og:url" content={Astro.url.href}/>
    <meta
            property="og:image"
            content={image}
    />
    {/* Basic Twitter Card tags */}
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/>
    <meta name="twitter:title" content={title}/>
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description}/>
    <meta
            name="twitter:image"
            content={image}
    />
    <link href={Astro.url.href} rel="canonical"/>
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)}/>
    <script defer src="https://cloud.umami.is/script.js" data-website-id={import.meta.env.UMAMI_ID}></script>
    <link rel="sitemap" href="/sitemap-index.xml"/>
    <script is:inline>
        (() => {
            const stored = localStorage.getItem("theme");
            const theme = stored === "light" || stored === "dark"
                ? stored
                : (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches ? "dark" : "light");
            document.documentElement.dataset.theme = theme;
        })();
    </script>
    {/*
        THE PRESS, HELD UNTIL THE PAGE ACTUALLY GOES.

        A press ends when the finger lifts, and the reader then waits with nothing on screen
        saying the tap landed — measured 376ms to first paint on a phone at Slow-4G with a
        warm cache, 788ms cold before the entrance was fixed, and unbounded on a worse
        connection. That gap is the reported defect: a visitor tapped the goal card's way out
        several times because the first tap looked ignored.

        This is the second script on the site and the reason it is script at all: no CSS state
        outlives the release. It sets one attribute; every declaration it triggers is authored
        beside the `:active` it twins, so with the script absent the site behaves exactly as
        it does today.

        BUBBLE PHASE, NOT CAPTURE, so `defaultPrevented` means something — a capture listener
        runs before anything could cancel the click and would hold a press for a navigation
        that was then called off.

        WHAT IT REFUSES, and each entry is a way to strand a control on a page that never
        leaves: a modified click (the reader wanted a new tab), a target that is not this
        frame — which is what excludes every bib, since Patch.astro opens Strava in a new tab,
        and all six icon controls — a download, a jump to an anchor on this very page, and a
        click something else has already handled.

        NOT REFUSED: a cross-origin link. Those really do leave, in this tab, and the two role
        cards' company names are the only ones on the site — the same two whose press ink is
        otherwise the slowest thing here.

        TWO RESETS, and the second is the one a first draft of this missed. `pageshow` covers
        the back button: bfcache restores the DOM as it was, attribute and all, so without it
        a reader who navigates away and comes back finds a control still pressed. The timer
        covers a navigation that is started and never completes — a cancelled load, an
        offline drop — which no event reports. 8s is past any load the reader would still be
        waiting through, and by then the browser is showing its own progress.
    */}
    <script is:inline>
        (() => {
            const HELD = "data-leaving";
            let timer = 0;
            const clear = () => {
                for (const el of document.querySelectorAll("[" + HELD + "]")) el.removeAttribute(HELD);
                clearTimeout(timer);
                timer = 0;
            };
            document.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
                if (event.defaultPrevented || event.button !== 0) return;
                if (event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.shiftKey || event.altKey) return;
                const anchor = event.target && event.target.closest && event.target.closest("a[href]");
                if (!anchor) return;
                if (anchor.target && anchor.target !== "_self") return;
                if (anchor.hasAttribute("download")) return;
                const to = new URL(anchor.href, location.href);
                if (to.hash && to.href.split("#")[0] === location.href.split("#")[0]) return;
                anchor.setAttribute(HELD, "");
                timer = setTimeout(clear, 8000);
            });
            addEventListener("pageshow", clear);
        })();
    </script>
</head>
<body
        class="bg-[var(--background)] md:min-h-screen flex flex-col justify-center items-center"
>
<slot/>
</body>
</html>

<style is:global>
    /*
     * THE PLATFORM'S OWN PRESS FLASH, PUT BACK.
     *
     * UnoCSS's preflight sets `-webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent` on `html, :host`, so
     * this site has been shipping with the one press affordance a touch browser gives you for
     * free switched off. Measured on the live DOM before this line: `rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)`. The
     * guidance every source agrees on is that removing it without putting something back is
     * the defect — and until this change one of the four link idioms had nothing back.
     *
     * The named states below it are the real answer and this is the floor under them: it fires
     * on anything tappable, including whatever gets added next without remembering to declare a
     * press, and including iOS, where the pseudo-class is unreliable for elements that are not
     * links or form controls. Blink and WebKit only — a browser without it falls through to
     * those named states, which is why they are the answer and this is the floor.
     *
     * The accent at 18% rather than a system default, so the flash belongs to this palette in
     * both themes; `--accent` is already theme-scoped, so this follows it with no second token.
     *
     * ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. This has the same specificity as the preflight rule it overrides,
     * so it wins only by coming later in the built sheet. tests/build-output.test.ts asserts
     * that rather than trusting the injection order, which is Astro's to change.
     */
    html {
        -webkit-tap-highlight-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 18%, transparent);
    }

    body {
        margin: 0;
        font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, "Oxygen", Ubuntu, Cantarell, "Fira Sans", "Droid Sans", Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
        -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
        background-color: var(--background);
        color: var(--text);
        transition: background-color 0.3s ease, color 0.3s ease;
    }

    b {
        font-weight: 700 !important;
    }

    /* Theme Variables
     *
     * Seven roles, deliberately separate tokens even where several of them resolve
     * to the same hex today:
     *
     *   --shadow          the offset plate cast by the portrait and the controls
     *   --accent          the interactive affordance: control border, hover ink
     *   --progress-fill   the *marked* region of a progress bar
     *   --progress-track  the unmarked remainder of that bar
     *   --status-live     the Now card's live indicator dot
     *   --status-halo     that dot's decorative pulsing halo
     *   --brand-ink       a brand-coloured glyph standing in for a word in prose
     *
     * Plus four for the patch wall, which are two roles seen from both sides:
     *
     *   --sport-ride      the cycling mark where it sits on a CARD
     *   --sport-ride-on-ink   the same mark on an INK-coloured surface
     *   --sport-run       the running mark on a card
     *   --sport-run-on-ink    the same mark on ink
     *
     * A PATCH IS DRAWN ON ONE OF TWO GROUNDS, and the sport colour has to follow the
     * ground rather than the theme. A finished bib is inverted — its face is --text —
     * so the mark on it needs the pale hue in the LIGHT theme. A booked bib has no
     * face at all and its mark sits on the card, where the light theme needs the DEEP
     * hue. Same sport, same theme, opposite value; the `-on-ink` half is always the
     * other half of the pair, which is why the dark block below is the light block
     * with each pair swapped and nothing else.
     *
     * That is not a hypothetical trap. It is exactly how --brand-ink came to sit at
     * 2.77:1 on a dark face earlier in this project: a token chosen against the card
     * and then painted on ink. The convention that surfaces it is the one
     * --on-brand already established — a `-on-` name states the surface it was chosen
     * against, and a token without one is for the card.
     *
     * Held to the 4.5:1 TEXT floor — text, not the 3:1 graphics floor, because the mark
     * is an icon AND the word beside it. Two instruments agree on all eight
     * combinations: `tests/patch-wall.test.ts` resolves the chain out of the shipped
     * stylesheet and does the arithmetic, and a scratchpad probe samples the composited
     * pixels of the rendered page. BE PRECISE ABOUT WHICH IS WHICH — an earlier draft
     * of this note said the test measures pixels. It does not, and it cannot: there is
     * no browser in the suite. That distinction is the whole reason the test also
     * carries a separate structural guard against a translucent ancestor, which is the
     * one thing arithmetic over authored colours can never see.
     *
     * --brand-ink is worn by exactly one thing, the footer heart, and it is NOT
     * --accent even though the two match in light mode. --accent is the
     * interactive affordance, and nothing about that heart responds to a pointer
     * or a key; re-toning what a control does on hover must not re-tone a word in
     * a sentence. That is the same coupling --progress-fill was split out to
     * break, so reaching for --accent here would rebuild it under a new name.
     * It is not --status-live either: that reports a live state, and a heart in
     * "Built with <heart>" reports nothing — it IS the word, which is why the
     * span beside it supplies "love" for a screen reader.
     *
     * Being a word rather than an ornament is also why it is toned as ink and
     * measured as text: 6.52:1 on the light card, 9.08:1 on the dark one, both
     * clear of the 4.5:1 that 12px prose asks for. And it follows the surface the
     * way --progress-fill does, deep on light and pale on dark, so the mark stays
     * a shade of the same brand red in both themes instead of inverting into a
     * different colour.
     *
     * The bar used to paint --shadow, so re-toning the plate silently re-toned
     * the data. Two colours matching in this palette is a coincidence of this
     * palette, not a constraint — collapsing them would rebuild that coupling
     * under a different name.
     *
     * POLARITY FOLLOWS THE SURFACE. The filled region is the figure and the
     * track is the ground, so --progress-fill flips with the theme (deep on
     * light, pale on dark) while --progress-track stays a quiet channel about
     * 1.5:1 from its card. Do NOT "improve" the bar by darkening the track in
     * light mode: that makes the *unfilled* remainder the strongest mark on the
     * card and inverts what the bar reports.
     *
     * --on-brand is the ink for content sitting ON --progress-fill, so it flips
     * with the fill. It is not a fixed dark: a dark icon on the light-mode fill
     * would be 2.77:1, the same class of failure it was introduced to fix.
     *
     * NOTHING WEARS IT TODAY. The progress bar became a 2px rule and its sport glyph went
     * with the capsule, so as of that change --on-brand has no consumer — it is KEPT rather
     * than deleted because the obligation it encodes returns the moment any ink is put back
     * on the fill, and the ratio above is the whole reason the token exists rather than a
     * literal. Read this paragraph in the conditional: it describes what the token is FOR,
     * not what the page currently does. See ProgressBar.astro, whose own note says the same
     * from the other side, and tests/build-output.test.ts, which fails if the bar regrows a
     * child without the ink check being restored.
     */
    :root[data-theme='light'] {
        --background: #FAFAFA; /* grey-50 */
        --card-background: #F5F5F5; /* grey-100 */
        --card-border: #E5E5E5; /* grey-200 */
        --shadow: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --accent: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --text: #0B0B0B; /* darkslate-900 */
        --progress-fill: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --progress-track: #E3B3B8; /* primary-150 */
        --status-live: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --status-halo: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --brand-ink: #A82334; /* primary-600 — 6.52:1 on the card */
        --on-brand: #FAFAFA; /* grey-50 — light ink, the fill is deep here */
        --sport-ride: #A82334; /* primary-600 — deep, for the card */
        --sport-ride-on-ink: #F3A3AA; /* primary-200 — pale, for the inverted bib face */
        --sport-run: #1F4E9C; /* marine-600 */
        --sport-run-on-ink: #9FC0F0; /* marine-200 */
    }

    :root[data-theme='dark'] {
        --background: #111111; /* darkslate-700 */
        --card-background: #171717; /* darkslate-500 */
        --card-border: #2C2C2C; /* darkslate-100 */
        --shadow: #F3A3AA; /* primary-200 */
        --accent: #F9CDD3; /* primary-100 */
        --text: #FAFAFA; /* grey-50 */
        --progress-fill: #F9CDD3; /* primary-100 */
        --progress-track: #462F32; /* primary-800 */
        --status-live: #F3A3AA; /* primary-200 */
        --status-halo: #F9CDD3; /* primary-100 */
        --brand-ink: #F3A3AA; /* primary-200 — 9.08:1 on the card */
        --on-brand: #0B0B0B; /* darkslate-900 — dark ink, the fill is pale here */
        --sport-ride: #F3A3AA; /* primary-200 — pale, for the card */
        --sport-ride-on-ink: #A82334; /* primary-600 — deep, for the inverted bib face */
        --sport-run: #9FC0F0; /* marine-200 */
        --sport-run-on-ink: #1F4E9C; /* marine-600 */
    }

    /* THE CONTROL ROW WRAPS, AND IT DOES NOT COUNT. Three hand-written width
       queries used to grant it 4/3/2/1 columns; they are deleted, and what
       replaces them is one declaration that cannot be tuned wrong.

       WHY THE LADDER WENT. A column count is an approximation of "how many fit",
       maintained by hand against bounds that had to be kept in step with the
       breakpoints by hand, and it approximated wrong twice in a row — first
       stopping at two columns, then needing a fourth rung at a bound (13rem) that
       took a fitted budget to derive. The mechanism is worth restating because it
       is the reason a count can never be right here: two controls plus their
       separation are a fixed 9rem, since the control's box is text-relative, but A
       CARD'S WIDTH IS NOT — a card grows vertically with its content and never
       horizontally, because it is as wide as the viewport allows. So past some
       reader text size any fixed count exceeds the card, this row held the intro
       card's copy column open at its own minimum width, and the card's clipping
       sheared the hero copy and the controls both.

       WHAT REPLACES IT IS AN INVARIANT, not a smaller number. A wrapping row's
       minimum content width is its LARGEST ITEM, not the sum of a row of them —
       every item here is one declared box, so that minimum is exactly one control
       at every viewport and every text size. The row can therefore never hold the
       copy column wider than one control, which is the defect class rather than an
       instance of it, and there is no bound left to keep in step with anything.

       TWO THINGS ARE NOW LOAD-BEARING that were not while this was a two-dimensional
       layout, and both are asserted in tests/control-geometry.test.ts rather than
       left to hold by luck. The control's own `shrink-0` stops the box being
       compressed below its declared width, which the ladder made irrelevant and
       this makes decisive again (uno.config.ts has the history — it once measured a
       control at 47.80px). And every child of this row has to BE a control: one
       child with a wider minimum width would restore exactly the defect above, so
       the row's minimum is only one control for as long as that holds.

       MEASURED, against the ladder it replaces rather than against the defect both
       of them fix. NAME THE EDGE AND THE GRID, because these are two different sweeps
       and an earlier draft of this note quoted one number for both: CONTROL BOXES past
       a card's right clip edge are 0 at all 32 configurations (4 widths x 8 root sizes),
       and past its BOTTOM clip edge 0 at all of a further 27 (9 viewports x 3 roots,
       desktop included); TEXT INK is 0 at 34 of its 35 (7 widths x 5 roots) at the right
       edge and 0 at all 35 at the bottom. The one exception is the documented residual,
       and it is not this row's doing: at 320 wide and a 40px root one unbreakable hero
       word overruns by 34.58, which is this card's own padding spending 120 of the 320.
       Deleting the rungs also removes the last two rem bounds that had no variant sibling
       to move with.

       WHAT THE LADDER'S ABSENCE USED TO COST is recorded here rather than by pointer,
       because the pointer was wrong — an earlier draft sent the reader to uno.config.ts,
       which never held these figures, so deleting them from this file recorded them
       nowhere. Control boxes sheared past the right clip edge by 142 at 320 wide and a
       40px root, 102 at 360, 87 at 375, 48 at 414, 50 at 320/root 32, 10 at 360/root 32
       and 4 at 320/root 28 — so the onset is a 28px root, not the 32 a text-only sweep
       reported. Text ink lost 136.84 / 96.84 / 81.84 / 42.84 at 320/360/375/414 at a 40px
       root, and 47.44 / 7.44 at 320/360 at a 32px root.

       IT CHANGES THE DEFAULT TEXT SIZE, which the ladder deliberately did not, and
       that is a design change carried on purpose rather than a side effect: the row
       now fits as many controls as there is room for instead of as many as a rung
       allows. At a 16px root, 360 and 375 wide go from 2 per row over 4 rows to 3
       over 3 (document height 1856 -> 1792 at 360, and 1836 -> 1772 at 375 — one pair
       does not describe both), 414 goes from 3 to 4, and 320 is unchanged at 2.
       13 of the 35 swept configurations get shorter, by up to 160px, and 2 get taller
       (on a finer 70-configuration grid it is 26 and 7 — see the next paragraph, and
       always say which grid a count came from).

       WIDER VIEWPORTS GAIN MOSTLY ONCE THE TEXT GROWS, not at the default size, and an
       earlier draft of this note had that wrong in a way worth recording: it said they
       "gain more, because the old top rung capped every one of them at 4". The rung did
       cap them, but the figures behind that sentence were measured before the portrait
       was pinned (`shrink-0` in IntroCard.astro), when this row was taking width from
       the photo — so they described a build that did not ship. Measured on what did:
       768 and 1024 are UNCHANGED at 4 per row at a 16px root, because the portrait now
       keeps its 275px and the copy column takes only what is left. The gains are real
       but they sit at larger text and at the widest viewports — 768 at a 20px root goes
       3 -> 6, 1024 at 20 goes 4 -> 5, 1024 at 24 goes 4 -> all seven on one row, and
       1440 at 16 goes 4 -> 5.

       SEVEN GET TALLER, and the reason is the whole trade rather than a wrinkle in it.
       An earlier draft said TWO, which was an artefact of sampling five root sizes where
       the clipping sweep samples eight — the same coarse-grid error that once put the
       shearing onset at a 32px root when it is 28. On a ten-root grid the page grows at
       375/28 (+434, the worst), 360/26 (+403), 414/28 (+385), 320/24 (+372), 375/26
       (+357), 360/24 (+330) and 320/22 (+264), it shrinks at 28 configurations by up to
       160, and 35 are identical. Every one of the seven is a narrow-to-mid viewport
       between 150% and 175% text zoom, where the row drops to one control per row and the
       ladder seated two. Measured, because
       the mechanism is not what it looks like: the ladder was not fitting two
       controls into the room available, it was DEMANDING room for two and getting
       it. Its minimum width of 216 held the copy column open to 216, and at that
       text size 216 still landed 42px inside the card's clip edge, so it fitted. A
       wrapping row demands nothing, so the copy column shrinks to the 174 actually
       on offer and two controls no longer fit it.

       That demand is not separable into a good half and a bad half — it is one
       behaviour. The same "hold the column open to N controls regardless of the
       room" that won 372px of height here is exactly what sheared 142px of BUTTON
       at 320 and a 40px root. Trading two configurations that grow and stay
       scrollable for a class of configurations that silently deleted functionality
       is the trade, stated plainly, and it is why this is the better engineering
       rather than merely the tidier CSS.

       Worth knowing for whoever wants that height back: below `md` the portrait is
       positioned out of flow, but its wrapper is still an in-flow sibling of the
       copy column and spends 48 of the card's 222 content-box pixels there. Those two
       figures were 24 and 198 in an earlier draft, DERIVED arithmetically instead of
       measured, and they were wrong in a way that inverted the conclusion: two controls
       at a 24px root need 216, which 222 accommodates and 198 does not. Recovering the
       48 would seat two controls at 320 and 360 wide at that root. That is a fix to the
       mobile hero, not to this row, and it is deliberately not made here.

       THE HONEST LIMIT IS UNCHANGED, because it never was the ladder's to fix: once
       ONE control plus the chrome between it and the viewport exceeds that viewport,
       the control itself is clipped and no layout here can help. That happens beyond
       roughly a 48px root on a 320px phone — past 300% zoom, comfortably clear of
       the 200% SC 1.4.4 asks for — and the test asserts that margin as a number so
       it cannot rot. Fixing it needs a control that shrinks or a card that scrolls
       sideways.

       THE SEPARATION CAN NO LONGER COST A PIXEL OF WIDTH — AND THAT IS NOT THE SAME AS
       SAFE. An earlier draft of this note claimed widening it "stopped being dangerous"
       and called that the clearest argument for this shape. That claim was measured on
       ONE EDGE and generalised to a class, which is the error this file warns about
       three paragraphs above, so it is corrected here rather than quietly dropped.

       What is true and measured: widened to 6rem under the column layout it pushed a
       control past the card's RIGHT clip edge at 12 of 32 configurations, worst 89px,
       12 of them at the DEFAULT text size; against this row it is 0 at all 32, because a
       separation only applies between items sharing a line and the minimum here is one
       item on a line by itself. So it can make this row taller and never WIDER.

       What is also true: taller is not free, because above `md` this card's height comes
       from its grid row and `overflow: hidden` clips the bottom too. The same 6rem
       widening puts 174px of control box past the BOTTOM clip edge at 1024x768 and hides
       the résumé control outright there and at 768x1024 and 1024x900 — with the gate
       green, because no assertion in this repo reads a bottom edge and a stylesheet test
       cannot lay out. The right-edge class closed; a bottom-edge one did not, and the
       browser sweep is the only thing that sees it.

       `justify-content` is deliberately absent: a wrapping row packs its items at
       the start of the line already, so declaring it restated the default. The
       ladder needed it to stop content-sized tracks stretching. */
    .control-row {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: 1rem; /* separates the controls from each other, on both axes */
    }

    /* Card entrance — replaces the JS stagger. Pure CSS, no client bundle.
       One rule per direct child of <main>: when a card is added, extend this
       ladder (a build-output test compares the ladder to the child count). */
    main > * {
        animation: card-in 0.4s ease-out backwards;
    }

    main > *:nth-child(1) { animation-delay: 0.00s; }
    main > *:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: 0.08s; }
    main > *:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: 0.16s; }
    main > *:nth-child(4) { animation-delay: 0.24s; }
    main > *:nth-child(5) { animation-delay: 0.32s; }
    main > *:nth-child(6) { animation-delay: 0.40s; }
    main > *:nth-child(7) { animation-delay: 0.48s; }
    main > *:nth-child(8) { animation-delay: 0.56s; }

    /*
     * NO FADE, AND A SHORT FIXED RISE. Both halves are measured, and the second exists only
     * because of what the first exposed.
     *
     * THE FADE WAS THE WAIT. Chromium does not record a contentful paint for a composited
     * opacity animation until it resolves, so a reader arriving here saw nothing legible until
     * this finished. Tap to full-contrast ink on a phone at Slow-4G: ~870ms before, ~500ms
     * after. (First-contentful-paint reads 788 -> 396ms cold, but quote that as FCP only — the
     * delta is exactly the 0.4s duration in both the cold and warm runs, which is the
     * signature of a duration rather than of a wait.)
     *
     * THE TRAVEL HAD TO SHRINK WITH IT, and this is the part a phone-only film could not show.
     * `translateY(40%)` is 40% of each child's OWN height, and `backwards` fill means a child
     * sits at the `from` keyframe for the whole of its delay. While that state was invisible
     * nobody paid for it; drawn at full ink it is the finished page rendered in the wrong
     * place. Measured on the built site at the first frame, at 1440x900 and 1024x797:
     *
     *     40% travel, faded    all six children invisible, displaced up to 282px, 327.6px clipped
     *     40% travel, no fade  all six at opacity 1, displaced up to 282px, 327.6px clipped
     *     12px travel, no fade all six at opacity 1, displaced 12px, NOTHING clipped
     *
     * The clipping is `main`'s own `overflow: hidden` (index.astro) and it was always there —
     * the fade was hiding it. A 430x932 film reported none of this because `main`'s second
     * child is `display: contents` there and children 3-6 are below the fold, so exactly one
     * box was animating.
     *
     * So the entrance survives, the ladder and the reduced-motion block below stay meaningful,
     * and the first frame is the page rather than a rehearsal of it. An absolute length rather
     * than a percentage is the point: it cannot scale with a card's height, which is what made
     * the 705px column move 282px.
     */
    @keyframes card-in {
        from {
            transform: translateY(12px);
        }
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        main > * {
            animation: none;
        }
    }
</style>
